# Step 5: the paths that always need a person.
#
# The article's rule is that any CI workflow file needs a human regardless of
# how small the diff is. A one-person repo cannot do that with an approval
# count, because GitHub will not let you approve your own pull request — so the
# count stays at 0 and this file carries the rule instead.
#
# The scope is deliberately narrow. These paths change rarely, so being an
# owner here costs almost no time. Widening it to the whole repo would make
# every pull request wait on someone else.

# CI can check everything except itself. A pull request can delete the test
# step and add failing code in the same diff, and CI comes back green.
/.github/workflows/ @dennisjhao

# The file that assigns ownership has to be owned, or a pull request can delete
# it first and change a workflow second. Same shape as the CLAUDE.md problem:
# the thing that governs review must not be editable by what is being reviewed.
/.github/CODEOWNERS @dennisjhao
